The tickless idle this firmware runs, recovered from 0x73ec8..0x7401f. Two
config bits carry the rest of the difference and live in
abi/config-relink/FreeRTOSConfig.h; what is here is what no switch produces.

1. The entry and exit times are read out of a loop that repeats until two
   consecutive reads of the RTC counter agree. The counter is clocked from
   LFCLK and a CPU read can catch it mid-increment.

2. The entry time is biased by the tick interrupt's own pending bit, sampled
   inside that loop (0x73f0a reads NVIC->ISPR[1], 0x73f22 takes bit 4, which is
   RTC2's IRQ 36). A tick already pending is a tick the kernel has not counted,
   so the sleep started one tick earlier than the counter says.

3. Waiting is a loop, not a single call, in both arms: sd_app_evt_wait()
   returns on events the SoftDevice handles and does not forward, and the image
   goes back to sleep unless NVIC has something pending (0x73f72..0x73f80 for
   the SoftDevice arm, 0x74008 for the bare one).

4. Between re-enabling the tick and sampling the exit time there is a 350
   iteration busy loop (0x73fa4, `movw r1, #0x41a` then `subs #3 / bpl`), about
   16 us, so a tick that is due arrives before the correction is computed
   rather than after it.

5. The correction itself is two-branch (0x73fd0). The SDK steps the whole
   elapsed count whatever happened. This one steps the whole count only when it
   woke early; when it slept the time it asked for, the last tick is delivered
   through xTaskIncrementTick() instead, so the scheduler sees it. That is the
   64 ticks a minute the SDK port loses.

Reading the counter through NVIC->ISPR and NVIC_ClearPendingIRQ rather than the
SoftDevice's wrappers is the image's own choice too; the critical region around
the whole function is sd_nvic_critical_region_enter/exit inlined, which is what
the SDK's header does by itself, minus APP_ERROR_CHECK on a call that cannot
fail here.
